---
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';
import { THUMBNAIL_HOSTS, NETWORK_LABELS, FACETS } from '../lib/data';
---
<TextPage
	title="Privacy Policy | eCamx"
	description="What eCamx collects (almost nothing), what third-party cam platforms collect when you view an embedded stream, and how to control it."
	heading="Privacy Policy"
	updated="19 August 2026"
>
	<p>
		Short version: eCamx is a static website. We do not ask you to register and
		we do not run advertising trackers. We do use Google Analytics to count
		visits, which sets a cookie — and if you are in the EU, the EEA or the UK,
		nothing analytics-related loads until you press <em>Accept &amp; continue</em>
		on the notice at the top. The embedded players from cam platforms are a
		different story — read on.
	</p>

	<h2>What eCamx collects</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>No account data.</strong> There is nothing to sign up for.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Google Analytics.</strong> We use Google Analytics 4 (measurement
			ID <code>G-JSSZP2XFBL</code>) to see how many people visit, which pages
			they open, and which parts of the site people actually use — which cam
			site and which button a click went to, whether a player was started, and
			whether a listing looked empty. It sets its own cookies in your browser
			and sends Google the page URL, your approximate location, and basic device
			and browser details. Per Google's documentation, Analytics 4 does not log
			or store IP addresses. <strong>Google Signals is switched off</strong> on
			this property, so your activity here is never joined to a Google account
			or used to build an advertising profile. We do not use Analytics for
			advertising or remarketing, we do not combine it with any other data, and
			we only ever look at aggregate numbers. See
			<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Google's privacy policy</a>.
			If you would rather not be counted, any content blocker or Google's own
			<a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">opt-out add-on</a>
			stops it, and the rest of the site keeps working normally.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Your choice about analytics.</strong> Visitors in the EU, the EEA
			and the UK are asked first: the notice at the top of the page offers
			<em>Accept &amp; continue</em> and <em>Essential only</em>, both the same
			size, and <strong>until you pick one, the Google Analytics library is not
			loaded at all</strong> — no cookie, no request, not even a cookieless one.
			Choosing <em>Essential only</em> keeps the whole site working; the only
			difference is that we do not count your visit. The choice is remembered in
			your browser's local storage under <code>ecamx-consent</code> and in a
			cookie of the same name, so clearing site data asks again. Outside those
			regions Analytics loads with the disclosure above and the opt-out options
			in it; we work out which case applies from your browser's time zone, and
			when we cannot read it we treat you as needing to be asked.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Counting clicks on our own server.</strong> When you press a button
			that leaves for a cam site, the request passes through
			<code>ecamx.com/go/…</code> on our own server, and we count it there as
			well as in your browser. The reason is honest and worth stating: content
			blockers stop the Google Analytics library for a large share of visitors,
			so browser-side counting alone tells us far less than it looks. The
			server-side count carries the cam site's name and which button was pressed
			— never the page you came from, and never your IP address. It uses a random
			identifier we store in a cookie called <code>ecamx_cid</code> so that
			repeat clicks from one browser are not counted as new people; it is not
			linked to anything else and it tells us nothing about who you are.
			<strong>This follows the same choice as above:</strong> if you picked
			<em>Essential only</em>, or you are in the EU, the EEA or the UK and have
			not chosen, the server sends nothing and stores no identifier.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>One local preference.</strong> If you dismiss the age notice, that
			choice is stored in your browser's local storage so it stays dismissed.
			It never leaves your device and we cannot read it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Standard server logs.</strong> Our host, Cloudflare, processes
			request metadata (IP address, user agent, requested URL) to serve the site
			and protect it from abuse. See
			<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>What third parties collect</h2>
	<p>
		This is the part that matters. When a page loads, your browser connects
		directly to the cam platform that hosts the stream and to its image servers.
		Those connections are between you and them — eCamx is not in the middle and
		cannot see or control what they do.
	</p>
	<p>
		Those platforms will typically set their own cookies, log your IP address,
		and may track you across sites. They are:
	</p>
	<ul>
		{FACETS.networks.map((n) => <li>{NETWORK_LABELS[n.slug]}</li>)}
	</ul>
	<p>
		Thumbnail images load from their content delivery networks, including
		{' '}{THUMBNAIL_HOSTS.slice(0, 6).join(', ')} and similar hosts. If you click
		through to a platform, that platform's privacy policy governs everything that
		happens next.
	</p>

	<h3>Third-party scripts</h3>
	<p>
		Apart from Google Analytics above, eCamx loads no third-party script. Until
		28 August 2026 Amateur.tv model pages ran a player script from cdn.a00s.net;
		it is gone, and those pages now show a preview image and a link instead.
	</p>

	<h3>BongaCams player</h3>
	<p>
		BongaCams model pages play the stream in our own video player, which fetches
		the video directly from BongaCams' content delivery network
		(<strong>bcvcdn.com</strong>). If the address we hold has expired, your
		browser asks our server for a fresh one and that request passes your IP
		address on to BongaCams, the same as loading any image from them would.
	</p>

	<h2>Affiliate links</h2>
	<p>
		Links from eCamx to cam platforms carry an affiliate identifier. If you sign
		up or spend money there, we may receive a commission. The identifier tells
		the platform the visit came from us; it does not tell us who you are.
		You pay nothing extra.
	</p>

	<h2>Your rights</h2>
	<p>
		Under the GDPR and similar laws you have the right to access, correct, and
		erase personal data held about you, and to object to its processing. Since we
		hold no personal data beyond transient server logs, in practice there is
		usually nothing for us to return or delete — but you are welcome to ask at
		<a href="mailto:info@ecamx.com">info@ecamx.com</a>. For data held by a
		cam platform, you must contact that platform directly.
	</p>

	<h2>Blocking third-party content</h2>
	<p>
		If you would rather not connect to the cam platforms at all, block
		third-party cookies and iframes in your browser settings, or use a content
		blocker. The directory pages will still work; the embedded players will not.
	</p>

	<h2>Changes</h2>
	<p>
		We will update the date at the top of this page when this policy changes.
	</p>
</TextPage>
